 mouths  in the in the the role that they would  have to play and that was something that  really struck me i don't even think  regina knows this  but in almost every casting of the stage  play  the first person usually cast is jim  brown  really no that's actually for every  production that's always been the anchor  around which everyone else gets cast  it's always jim brown first  look at that great minds think alike  wow that's that's really that's some  special and  it is right thank you for sharing that  kim  no i'll get to say that and then all  right  rest of your jungles you can say so kemp  said  i love that let's flip over to the sound  design real quick um  tyson terrell again says i found it very  intimate the sound design especially  during the boxing scene  which is very different from what is  traditional for boxing scenes  all also not much music that i noticed  until the near end of the second act  what were your thoughts  as you created the soundscape thank you  so much  well um they're there there there is  he's correct  uh tyson um that  that there is not a lot of music  but again i with dialogue this powerful  i'm really not a fan of music stepping  on dialogue  um it rubs me kind of  uneasy as an audience member so  definitely i wouldn't be making that  choice as a director  with with this uh piece especially  but um part of it is also pandemic  related  um when it came down to doing the score  we i we we didn't have that that  wonderful  opportunity that you normally have where  you get to sit  with your composer and have these great  conversations  and then go into the studio and and  you know figure it out with artists that  was not an  option um for us and  uh so uh along the way  we just kind of made the discovery that  um  let's let piano be a fifth character  let's let's let the because there were  this when i kind of put my playlist  together  um and only one song is actually in the  film  but for when it comes to  uh film i feel like probably a lot of  directors  can relate to and writers as well can  relate to  you have just certain songs or sounds  that are inspiring that feel like they  connect  to the story and for me donnie hathaway  and nina simone  uh just her arrangement the bluesiness  that nina has um and  and a few other blues songs were part of  my playlist that i put together in one  night in miami and one of the thing that  was very common  in that playlist is the piano you know  and so uh we we made a decision  to uh go uh with the  pretty much piano score throughout  except for  um there's one prayer scene where we  again it's a solo  uh instrument with we use the duke and  um uh and benny green  flew in from uh uh northern california  and recorded all of those uh piano uh  moments that  take us from the first fight in uh  um in uh london  to uh the coppa and from the  fight from uh miami  to say sam pulling up you know so  there are uh the music does become a bit  of a  character so much so that you know i  guess  maybe some may have missed it in places  that's hilarious you're like well maybe  um  i want to ask this question i tried to  get to let me just say this i tried to  get to everyone's questions and some of  them were very similar  and or regina and kemp answered them so  if i didn't call your name out or call  the  question out in particular it's because  they had already answered it i'm not  dissing y'all i'm just trying to  expedite the process so  having said that um there is a question  about  what involvement if any did jim brown  and the estates of muhammad ali  malcolm x and sam cook have with the  film  well jim brown's daughter is actually  in the film so that answered that  yes um i had  conversations with um  uh may may ali leila ali and also  ambassador shabazz  um and uh just  because i knew all of those women prior  to  doing the film i just kind of wanted to  let them know that i was doing this and  that would be great to have their  blessing  and i understand just their their  fathers being who  their fathers are and and how much they  mean to  them let alone the rest of the world  um i understand if they had any  you know thoughts or feelings going in  um but  maymay and layla had actually seen the  play so they were  aware of you know the story that we were  telling  and and all of them had given me their  blessing and all of them  uh didn't uh try to  they shared their honest feelings about  um how dear their fathers are  to them and you know i assure them that  my intention is not to do anything  uh uh other than  uh pay respect to the men that they are  and um my conversations with ambassador  shabazz  you know her being the eldest of um  malcolm's children uh she definitely  shared a couple little anecdotes that um  we ended up infusing yeah we wrote them  as soon as regina told me i'm like  i'm writing that right into the movie  yeah  and he was like she did say it was okay  i was like yup  this is the perfect example how much  kemp and i would be on the same page  so i'm having the conversation with  ambassador shabazz and she's  sharing this and i'm like oh my god  because it's a moment that i'm like  we forget that malcolm's a father a a  a husband and and kemp has already  uh given us more  um than than i think we've  seen before as far as just  the the man that's not the great orator  that's not the great  um motivator um  and so what um ambassador  shared with us was just an extension of  that  and and so i immediately was like  i better ask her is she okay with this  and  i said do you think can i can include  this  in the film how would you feel she was  like sure sure  i i just i she said i hate to watch  films that people have access to the  people  that um were part of those lives  and they don't talk to them  and um i uh thought that  you know that was really interesting  because i think a lot of films  probably people do want to talk to the  people but uh  it financially it doesn't really work  out  if they can and she was  a person that wasn't looking for money  she just  um was um speaking to  uh um the last time i'd seen her i was  16 years old  and and last time i'd spoken to her 16  years old so she was just  blessing this 16 year old girl that she  that now is a woman that she was proud  of and and  was just sharing so and for those who  don't know the scene it's  when malcolm calls his daughter that's  ambassador shabazz  he did leave notes for her hidden in  books so  we we i wrote that's that moment because  it was something that she told us about  wow you guys have dropped so many little  kernels of nuggets and some  information that i certainly didn't know  going into the film or having seen it as  i told regina  right before we went live that i've seen  the movie five times and i'll probably  see it again  but um unfortunately we have to end it  right now  regina king you are the bomb.com and i'm  speaking it into existence this film  is gonna get all kinds of love this  awards season and i am there for all of  it  kemp powers these two films that you  have  seoul and one night in miami honoring us  brother thank you from your howard  university  bison sister thank you from the bottom  of my heart and thank both of you for  joining us today for this film  independent discussion  on one night in miami i am your  moderator  carla renata aka the curvy critic and i  will  see y'all next time bye regina and kim
 

